# /weekly-wrapup — review the week from local records
Use the shared `runtime/work_report.js` core for the week containing the
requested date. This command is one convenience surface for a weekly report;
the workflow that calls it remains responsible for the request and completion
decision. The historical command name is a compatibility label; other workflows
may call the same core directly. Without a date, use the current local date and
state the local timezone. The core searches records and prepares a recoverable
report; Google Drive, Sheets, email, or another collaboration space is a later
publication choice.
## Sequence
1. Run the local collector with `--period weekly --date YYYY-MM-DD`. The period
is Monday through Sunday. Include Git commits, changed or created files,
and any explicitly configured session export; preserve warnings for sources
that could not be read.
2. Read `evidence.jsonl`, `manifest.json`, and the evidence judgment reference.
Deduplicate repeated traces without strengthening their meaning. A clone,
build output, commit title, or session heading may explain a lead but cannot
establish completed work, approval, delivery, authorship, or importance.
3. Write a concise Korean `report.md` with observed facts, interpretations tied
to evidence IDs, confirmation questions, and the next useful action. An
empty or partial source means “no confirmed record” or “collection warning”,
never “no work”.
4. Show the local draft before any external write. Ask which destination, account,
and target should receive it. Do not assume a Google Sheet or a fixed row or
column exists.
5. If Google is selected, continue with `jstack:google-workspace`; otherwise use
the selected sink's contract. Record the destination receipt separately and
retain the local report when publication fails.
```bash
node "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/runtime/work_report.js" \
--period weekly --date "${1:-$(date +%F)}"
```
The report core uses semantic fields. A destination adapter may map them to its
own headers or properties, but C/D-style physical addresses never belong in
the core and do not determine whether the weekly report is complete.
